<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-war-of-1551-1559">Italian War of 1551–1559</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis consisted of treaties signed in April 1559 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, ending more than sixty years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. France renounced its Italian claims but kept Calais and the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy">Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy</a> recovered most of his duchy. The settlement left Spain dominant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for over a century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-cateau-cambresis.jpg" alt="Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cateau-Cambresis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a> and the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> began in 1494 and lasted over sixty years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> abdicated in January 1556 and divided his possessions: the Habsburg lands went to his brother Ferdinand, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> ruled the Spanish Netherlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> added. Philip's victories at St Quentin in 1557 and Gravelines in 1558 let him negotiate from strength, but he could no longer pay his troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> was also ready for peace, having occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> in 1552 and retaken Calais in January 1558, and divisions caused by the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> weakened his nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The settlement consisted of two treaties: one between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 2 April 1559 and one between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 3 April, both approved by Emperor Ferdinand I. Henry II gave up his claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, recognised Philip II as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and of the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and ceded the Presidi to Spain. Spain gave back Saint Quentin, Ham and Le Catelet in northern France.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and restored the Duchy of Savoy to Emmanuel Philibert, who married Henry's sister Margaret. France kept five fortresses there, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinerolo">Pinerolo</a> and Chivasso. Henry also kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>. England granted France the Pale of Calais for eight years, a mechanism to save face, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, who had succeeded Mary I in November 1558, was formally recognised as queen. Calais stayed French afterwards. Philip II married Henry's daughter Elisabeth of Valois.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Henry II was injured in a jousting accident during the tournament held to celebrate the peace and died on 10 July 1559. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> died in December 1560 and was succeeded by the 10-year-old Charles. Political instability and the demobilisation of thousands of unpaid troops led to the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a> in 1562.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was left divided between Spanish viceroyalties in the south and imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in the north, among them Tuscany under the Medici, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> under the Spanish Habsburgs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> under Savoy. Spain's control of Milan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> was confirmed, and the division lasted until the wars of succession of the 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s loss of Calais, its last stronghold on the Continent, damaged its reputation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1535 it became a province of the Habsburg monarchy, confirmed to Spain by the <b>Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</b> in 1559, and its revenues and soldiers served wars fought elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Emmanuel Philibert, serving Philip II of Spain, commanded the Spanish army that crushed the French at the Battle of Saint-Quentin in 1557, and the <b>Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</b> in 1559 restored most of his lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II’s forces defeated France at Saint-Quentin in 1557 and Gravelines in 1558, leading to the <b>Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</b> in 1559.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis</b> between France and Spain in 1559 withdrew French support, and Montalcino surrendered to Cosimo I de' Medici, joining the future Grand Duchy of Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-siena-at-montalcino">Republic of Siena at Montalcino</a></li></ul></section>
